+    ## One document, passed through (v0.4)
+
+    The HomeInventoryInput the page posts is byte-for-byte the document the
+    calculation container reads, and the PackageDefinition is the document that
+    describes the work. The service stores, hashes and forwards it; it
+    transforms nothing. That is what makes the content hash cover exactly what
+    was simulated, and it is why there is no second, calculation-side schema
+    that can drift away from this one. A field the calculation does not
+    simulate is fine inside the document: the calculation's translation report
+    says so for that field rather than the contract hiding it.
+
+    The user's answers are the input, and the calculation derives the physics.
+    Categorical descriptors are the primary fields (walls.construction,
+    walls.insulation, windows.type), each with an EXPERT OVERRIDE beside it
+    (u_value_in_watt_per_m2_per_kelvin, area_in_m2) that wins when it is
+    present. No field asks a homeowner for an internal of the calculation:
+    v0.3's tabula_building_type, building_heat_capacity_class and
+    travel_route_set are gone, replaced by the questions a form can ask
+    (dwelling_type, construction and insulation per element,
+    commuting_distance_in_km), from which the calculation derives the TABULA
+    code, the fabric heat capacity and the travel profile.
+
+    ## Stages (v0.4)
+
+    A package carries a schedule, and a schedule makes a package a sequence
+    rather than a single state. Stage k of a package is the home inventory with
+    the measures of phases 1..k applied, so stage 0 is the dwelling as it
+    stands and the last stage is the finished renovation. There is ONE
+    calculation and one result PER STAGE, read from
+    /homeinventories/{hi_id}/packages/{pkg_id}/stages/{k}/detailed-simulation;
+    the roadmap the user sees is composed from those results. A stage's start
+    year escalates the economics and dates the installations. It does not
+    change the weather: every stage is simulated against the same country
+    dataset, so two stages differ by what was built and by nothing else.
+
